From 3ed74004d8a8c5256d76680c3297e8fd4a1e032f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hector Isaza <76170012+hecisaza@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 21:14:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] server: a DSML value that holds its block's closer keeps its
 call, streamed live or held

DeepSeek-V4.1 writes DSML parameter values unescaped, so a value can hold its block's own closer (a file about DSML).
tool_parse ended a block at its first closer, inside that value: the call was lost and the tags after the value stayed in
the reply's text. The parse now reads a reply two ways, as 0052 of the 4-Spark recipe does: its blocks ended at their
first closer, and its blocks ended where reply_text.blockEnd ends them (past a closer that lies inside a parameter value,
as the parser reads values). It takes the second reading only when that gives strictly more calls after the call limit,
so the reading taken never has fewer calls; on a tie, and for a block the walker cannot read, the first closer still ends
the block. A reply whose blocks read the same both ways parses exactly as before.

TENSORFOLD_TOOL_STREAM picks how a stream with tools sends DSML calls; it is read once at startup (unset, empty or blank:
live; otherwise exactly live or hold, else the server refuses to start).

live (the default) keeps 0048's live streaming: a call's header when its invoke name is whole, string values as they
decode. The streamer now ends a block where reply_text.blockEnd ends it: at a closer inside a value it sends the pieces up
to that closer, waits until the text says where the block ends, and streams the rest of the value; what completes such a
block's calls (each call's closing brace, the block's later calls) waits for the reply's end and goes out only when the
parse's first calls are, byte for byte, the calls that went out, so such a block never hands a client a completed call
the parse does not return. Ordinary blocks complete as soon as they close. The stream filter and the round's opener searches go on from
where the last round stopped. The
search for the closer is incremental, as before. A stream that hits max_tokens ends as length (Messages max_tokens,
Responses incomplete) whatever calls it sent, and a call whose arguments the parse rejects is left open, never completed.
Where a stream still differs from the whole reply (a reply cut inside a call, a misformatted block, a repeated
parameter) is the same set of cases as before this patch.

hold sends the prose as it decodes up to the first opener the stream filter knows (DSML blocks, <tool_call>,
<|tool_call>), and when the reply ends the rest of the content and then the calls: exactly the content and calls the
whole reply has, at the cost of the call's first bytes arriving at the reply's end.

Both modes: the keep-alive ping counts silence from what the client last got, so a long wait gets pings, and
/v1/responses has the ping too. Requests without tools and non-streamed replies are the same in both modes.

Tests: tool_parse_test.zig (values that hold their block's tags in both spellings, ties, call limits);
dsml_stream_test.zig (live: the chat, Responses and Messages routes over HTTP, every two-round split, a closer inside a
value at every split, finish reasons and content on replies cut by max_tokens, an end token or a stop string, text sent
at most once, a 256 KiB value streamed in 16-byte rounds within a time bound); dsml_fuzz_test.zig (6,000 random
well-formed replies whose values hold the block's closer stream the parse's calls byte for byte); dsml_hold_test.zig
(hold: the streams of the hold-only build, event for event over 312 replies, endings, routes and round sizes, against
testdata/hold_golden.txt; how to regenerate it is in the test); serve.zig (the setting's accepted values).
